  window.BIBLE_ENTRIES.push.apply(window.BIBLE_ENTRIES, [{"day": 51, "name": "Dan", "book": "Genesis 30, 49", "answer": "Dan", "color": "#c97a2c", "facts": ["My name means \"judge\" or \"he judged.\"", "I was born to Rachel's servant, Bilhah, though Jacob was my father.", "One of the twelve tribes of Israel is named after me, and it marks the end of our Part 1 journey through the Old Testament!"], "story": "Dan was one of Jacob's twelve sons, born to Bilhah, the servant of Rachel, at a time when Rachel longed for children of her own. His name, meaning \"judge,\" reflected his mother's hope that God would bring justice to her situation. Like his brothers, Dan became the founder of one of the twelve tribes of Israel, each carrying forward a piece of the larger story that began all the way back with his great-grandfather Abraham — a fitting figure to close out Part 1 of our journey through the Old Testament.", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.511}, {"day": 52, "name": "Saul", "book": "1 Samuel 9–31", "answer": "Saul", "color": "#8a3a2c", "facts": ["I became Israel's very first king.", "The Bible describes me as standing head and shoulders above everyone else.", "I was actually out looking for lost donkeys the day I was anointed king!"], "story": "Saul was a tall, impressive young man out searching for his father's lost donkeys when the prophet Samuel unexpectedly anointed him as Israel's first king. Saul had early successes leading Israel in battle, but over time, jealousy and fear — especially toward the popular young warrior David — began to take over his decisions. Saul's reign is often remembered as a story of promising beginnings overshadowed by choices driven by pride and insecurity.", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.511}, {"day": 53, "name": "Cain", "book": "Genesis 4", "answer": "Cain", "color": "#6b4a2c", "facts": ["I was the firstborn son of Adam and Eve.", "I was a farmer who grew crops from the soil.", "God gave me a special protective mark after I was sent away."], "story": "Cain was the first child ever born, the son of Adam and Eve, and he grew up to farm the land. When he let jealousy and anger take over after an argument about offerings to God, he made a terrible choice that hurt his brother Abel. God still showed Cain mercy, giving him a mark of protection even as he had to leave home. His story is one of the Bible's earliest lessons about controlling anger before it controls you.", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.511}, {"day": 54, "name": "Michal", "book": "1 Samuel 18–19, 25; 2 Samuel 6", "answer": "Michal", "color": "#8a2c4a", "facts": ["I was King Saul's daughter and David's very first wife.", "I helped David escape by lowering him out of a window and placing a decoy in his bed.", "I watched from a window as the Ark of the Covenant danced joyfully when it was brought into Jerusalem."], "story": "Michal was King Saul's daughter and fell in love with the young hero David, becoming his first wife. When her father's jealousy turned dangerous and he sent soldiers to kill David, Michal bravely helped her husband escape through a window, buying him time by placing a decoy in his bed. Her story shows the difficult position she was in — caught between loyalty to her father and love for her husband — throughout a turbulent period in Israel's history.", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.511}, {"day": 55, "name": "Laban", "book": "Genesis 29–31", "answer": "Laban", "color": "#7a5a2c", "facts": ["I was Rebekah's brother, making me Jacob's uncle.", "I tricked Jacob into marrying my older daughter Leah before he could marry Rachel.", "Jacob worked for me for twenty years, tending my flocks."], "story": "Laban was Rebekah's brother, and years later, his nephew Jacob showed up at his home looking for a fresh start. Laban gave Jacob work tending his flocks — but also gave him a taste of his own trickery, switching daughters on Jacob's wedding day. Despite the tension between them over the years, Laban's story is woven tightly into Jacob's own, shaping the family that would eventually become the twelve tribes of Israel.", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.511}, {"day": 56, "name": "Samson", "book": "Judges 13–16", "answer": "Samson", "color": "#a68a2c", "facts": ["I once tore apart a lion with my bare hands.", "I defeated a thousand enemy soldiers using only the jawbone of a donkey.", "My incredible strength came from a special vow I made to God called the Nazirite vow."], "story": "Samson was set apart before birth to be a judge and deliverer for Israel, given incredible superhuman strength as part of a special vow to God that included never cutting his hair. He performed astonishing feats of strength throughout his life, but he also made some risky choices, including falling for a woman named Delilah, who eventually discovered the secret behind his strength. Though his story includes real failures, Samson's final act brought down an enemy temple, delivering one last great victory for Israel.", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.511}, {"day": 57, "name": "Rehoboam", "book": "1 Kings 12", "answer": "Rehoboam", "color": "#6a3a8a", "facts": ["I was King Solomon's son and inherited his throne.", "I rejected wise advice from experienced elders in favor of harsher advice from friends my own age.", "My poor decision led to Israel splitting into two separate kingdoms."], "story": "Rehoboam inherited the throne from his father, Solomon, but when the people asked him to lighten the heavy burdens Solomon had placed on them, Rehoboam made a costly mistake. Ignoring the wise counsel of older, experienced advisors, he instead listened to younger friends who urged him to be even harsher. This decision led ten of Israel's twelve tribes to break away, splitting the once-unified kingdom into two — a turning point that shaped the rest of Israel's history.", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.511}, {"day": 58, "name": "Joash", "book": "2 Kings 11–12", "answer": "Joash", "color": "#2c6a8a", "facts": ["I became king of Judah at just seven years old.", "I was hidden in the temple as a baby to protect me from a dangerous relative who wanted the throne.", "I organized the repair of the Temple in Jerusalem during my reign."], "story": "Joash's life began in danger — as a baby, he was secretly hidden away in the Temple by his aunt to protect him from a relative who had seized the throne and tried to eliminate every royal heir. Raised secretly by the faithful priest Jehoiada, Joash was crowned king at just seven years old once it was safe. Guided by good advisors in his early years, Joash led important repairs to the Temple in Jerusalem, restoring it after years of neglect.", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.511}, {"day": 59, "name": "Judah", "book": "Genesis 29, 37, 43–44, 49", "answer": "Judah", "color": "#8a5a2c", "facts": ["I was Jacob's fourth son, born to Leah.", "One of the twelve tribes of Israel — and the whole region of \"Judea\" — is named after me.", "King David and Jesus both came from my family line."], "story": "Judah was Jacob's fourth son, and while he made a painful mistake in the plot against his brother Joseph, he later showed real growth — even offering to sacrifice his own freedom to protect his youngest brother Benjamin. That change of heart marked a turning point for the whole family. Generations later, Judah's family line would produce King David, and eventually, Jesus himself.", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.511}, {"day": 60, "name": "Jezebel", "book": "1 Kings 16–21; 2 Kings 9", "answer": "Jezebel", "color": "#8a1c2c", "facts": ["I was a foreign princess who married King Ahab of Israel.", "I promoted the worship of the false god Baal throughout the land.", "My name is still used today to describe someone manipulative or wicked."], "story": "Jezebel was a foreign princess who married King Ahab of Israel and brought her own religious practices with her, actively promoting the worship of the false god Baal throughout the kingdom. She clashed fiercely with the prophet Elijah and is remembered in the Bible as one of its most notorious figures for her role in leading Israel away from God. Her story serves as a serious warning about the far-reaching effects of poor influence and unchecked power.", "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "ratio": 0.511}]);
